60: \emph{framed} surgery manifold, including its meridian line. Let
61: \[
62:  N_e=(S^6\setminus\operatorname{int}(S^3\times D^3))
63:                   \cup(D^4\times S^2)
64: \]
65: use precisely the preceding normal frame. Along the Seifert collar,
66: the vector from its boundary into \(V\) is \(-\nu _1\).
67: Consequently the truncated Seifert surface has boundary
68: \(S^3\times\{-\nu _1\}\), and caps by the literal constant section
69: \(D^4\times\{-\nu _1\}\). Denote this closed four-manifold by
70: \(\widehat V\).
71: 
72: Here are the orientations of this cap. Write the ambient orientation
73: near the knot as
74: \(\Omega_{S^3}\wedge dn_1\wedge dn_2\wedge dn_3\).
75: The Seifert orientation is
76: \(dn_1\wedge\Omega_{S^3}\), so its oriented normal two-plane is
77: \(-dn_2\wedge dn_3\). At \(-\nu _1\), the outward-first
78: orientation of the normal meridian sphere is also
79: \(-dn_2\wedge dn_3\). The boundary of the knot complement is
80: \(+\Omega_{S^3}\wedge\Omega_{S^2}\); the surgery cap therefore
81: has orientation \(-\Omega_{D^4}\wedge\Omega_{S^2}\).
82: The cap of \(\widehat V\) has orientation \(-\Omega_{D^4}\).
83: Thus its oriented normal plane is exactly the positive meridian
84: plane, with no sign change in its Euler class.
85: 
86: Let \(x\) be the meridian class with integral one on this sphere.
87: The Mayer--Vietoris map in degree two identifies the boundary
88: \(H_2(S^3\times S^2)=\mathbb Z\) with the meridian summands in
89: both pieces; it gives \(H^2(N_e)=\mathbb Zx\). In degree four,
90: the connecting map identifies \([\widehat V]\) with the oriented
91: boundary \([S^3]\). The normal-plane calculation above therefore
92: gives the actual duality identity
93: \[
94:        \operatorname{PD}_{N_e}(x)=[\widehat V],\qquad
95:        x|_{\widehat V}=e(\nu\widehat V).                 \tag{SP.5}
96: \]
